{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790538748,"nonce":"3X1eERM0N8p+hpkwHoh2bYL7i3A=","title":"wasm: every built-in module on wasm (real where the sandbox allows, simulated or stubbed where not)","message":"fs, os, hash, crypto, zip, http, socket, proc, thread and isolate compile only with the host feature (src/runtime/core/mod.rs), so neither wasm build has them: the embedded runtime (wlift.js) reports \"Could not load module\", and wasm AOT binds 0 (2a036da). Both wasm builds get all of them, following Ash's wasm model (ash docs/wasm/README.md, host-abi.md, internals/wasm-fibers.md, wasm-threads.md).\n\n1. Pure computation, real on wasm. hash (sha1, sha2, md5, hmac, base64), crypto (aes-gcm, argon2, ed25519, password-hash) and zip (deflate) are pure Rust. They move out of the host feature into one that both wasm builds enable. Randomness comes from getrandom: WASI random_get, crypto.getRandomValues in the browser. zstd is C, so it stays out where there is no C toolchain for the target.\n2. fs and os. Under WASI (wasm AOT under wasmtime, or a browser behind a WASI shim), std::fs and std::env are real and scoped to the host's preopens. The embedded browser build gets an in-memory filesystem, seeded from what the page gives it, and os answers from the page (platform \"wasm\", no environment, args from the host).\n3. Through the host. socket takes Ash's twelve ash_host_socket_* imports verbatim. wasmtime answers them over libc, and a browser host answers the client half over WebSocket and refuses bind/listen/accept. http goes through a host import (fetch in a page, a native client under wasmtime). proc has no sandbox equivalent: every call fails with a clear \"not available on wasm\" error, and the module loads.\n4. Fibers in compiled wasm. Today a Fiber.yield inside AOT-compiled wasm raises (a_yield_in_compiled_code_raises). ash_wasm_link, which wasm AOT already links with, has the fiber transform (LinkOptions::fibers, seeded from env.ash_host_fiber_yield, globals ash_fiber_state/data/resume). Route WrenLift's suspension through that import and link with fibers on, so compiled bodies suspend and resume. thread needs this.\n5. thread, as Ash does it. On one agent: Thread.create runs on the caller's scheduler; a Mutex/Lock/Deque wait suspends the fiber and a release resumes it; nothing runs at the same time. Then on agents (wasm32-wasip1-threads, shared memory): a thread per agent where the host gives one, and as a fiber on the main scheduler where it does not.\n6. isolate: 7f446e8, an agent per isolate, each a fresh instance of the program.\n\nRelated: d51592b (the embedded runtime's scheduler and threads), 7f446e8, 2a036da.","files":null}]}